1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Ten red and ten green paper worms are scattered on grass.

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:08,256
Students act as hungry birds and pick up as many worms as they can in one minute.

3
00:00:08,256 --> 00:00:11,794
Predict: which colour will survive better? Pause and decide.

4
00:00:12,853 --> 00:00:18,043
Watch. After 30 seconds, most of the red worms have been found, but only one green worm.

5
00:00:19,102 --> 00:00:23,879
After one minute, just one red worm is left, but eight green worms have survived.

6
00:00:24,938 --> 00:00:26,235
Explain what you saw.

7
00:00:26,235 --> 00:00:31,484
The green worms are camouflaged against the grass, so the 'birds' find them less easily.

8
00:00:31,484 --> 00:00:37,204
In a real population, the better-camouflaged individuals survive, breed and pass on their genes,

9
00:00:37,204 --> 00:00:43,514
so over many generations more of the population has that variation. Pause and explain it in your own words.

10
00:00:44,573 --> 00:00:47,816
A population of mice has light grey and dark grey fur.

11
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:54,244
Owls hunt at night and spot the light mice more easily. Dark mice survive, breed and pass on their dark fur.

12
00:00:54,244 --> 00:00:58,608
Over generations, almost all the mice are dark. This is natural selection.

13
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:04,562
Darwin based this theory on Galápagos finches, whose beaks suited different foods.

14
00:01:04,562 --> 00:01:07,746
Individuals vary, and more are born than can survive.

15
00:01:07,746 --> 00:01:13,113
Those with favourable variations survive and pass them on. Over time, new species can form.

16
00:01:14,172 --> 00:01:18,005
Natural selection can be fast in species that reproduce quickly.

17
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:22,900
In a population of bacteria, one may carry a variation that resists an antibiotic.

18
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:27,677
The antibiotic kills the rest, and the resistant ones multiply into 'super bugs'.

19
00:01:28,735 --> 00:01:34,633
Insecticides work the same way. A few mosquitoes have genes to resist them; they survive and breed,

20
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:40,412
so over generations the insecticide on bed nets stops working. That answers our malaria question!

21
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:42,476
New insecticides must be developed.
